% APPENDIX A: Evaluation Prompts
% ================================================================
\phantomsection
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{\bf \uppercase{APPENDIX A: EVALUATION PROMPTS (RQ2 PROTOCOL)}}
\stoptocwriting
\chapter{\uppercase{Evaluation Prompts (RQ2 Protocol)}}
\resumetocwriting

This appendix reproduces the exact system and user prompts used by the GPT-4o evaluator (temperature 0.0, JSON mode) for the RQ2 multi-backbone evaluation (100 dialogues per condition across four LLM backbones). The evaluator was fully blind to the experimental condition; neither prompt reveals which backbone or condition generated the therapist responses. The same prompts were used for all 1,600 sessions. The appendix also reproduces the Layer-3 pairwise preference prompts and documents the cross-family second-judge protocol (Claude Sonnet 4.6), which reuses the identical pairwise prompts under the same two-order swap rule.

Two clarifications on the relationship between these prompts and the MIRROR evaluation framework \cite{kim-etal-2025-mirror}. First, the quoted constructs ``Emotional Subtext'' and ``Latent Concerns'' are our own operational definitions, written into the judge prompt to direct attention to the say-versus-feel gap the thesis targets; they are not verbatim MIRROR terminology. What MIRROR (via the COUNSELINGEVAL framework of \cite{lee2024cactus}) supplies is the scoring dimensions and scales: the CTRS-derived skill items rated 0--6 and the WAI-based alliance dimension rated 1--5. Second, our pointwise rubric retains the \emph{Strategy} item from the full six-item CACTUS/COUNSELINGEVAL set, whereas MIRROR excluded it after documenting a GPT-4o length bias in that dimension. We retain it deliberately, with two mitigations: the pairwise prompt explicitly forbids rewarding length, and all pairwise cells were audited post hoc for length bias ($|r(\Delta\text{length},\text{outcome})| \leq 0.17$; the largest win occurs with \emph{shorter} dissonance transcripts).

\subsection*{System Prompt}

\begin{quote}\small
You are an expert psychological evaluator specializing in Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) and therapeutic alliance. Your task is to analyze a full counseling session transcript and score the therapist's performance based on the evaluation frameworks used in the MIRROR paper (arXiv:2504.13211v2).

CRITICAL EVALUATION FOCUS:
\begin{itemize}
    \item You must assess the therapist's ability to perceive ``Emotional Subtext'': the underlying feelings that may not be explicitly stated in the client's words but are hinted at through context, tone, or provided emotional metadata.
    \item A high-performing therapist should move beyond surface-level reflection and identify ``Latent Concerns'' to facilitate deeper discovery.
\end{itemize}
You must evaluate the entire session as a whole, not just individual turns.
\end{quote}

\subsection*{User Prompt}

The placeholder \texttt{\{CONVERSATION\_TEXT\_GOES\_HERE\}} is replaced with the full session transcript (client and therapist turns concatenated as \texttt{``CLIENT: \ldots''} and \texttt{``THERAPIST: \ldots''}).

\begin{quote}\small
Please evaluate this therapy session. Pay close attention to how the therapist navigates the gap between what the client SAYS and what the client FEELS

[Session Transcript]
\{CONVERSATION\_TEXT\_GOES\_HERE\}
[/Session Transcript]

1. \textbf{Therapist Skills (0--6):} Understanding, Interpersonal Effectiveness\\
2. \textbf{Client Alliance (1--5):} Affective Bond\\
3. \textbf{CTRS (0--6 each):} Collaboration, Guided Discovery, Focus, Strategy

Return EXACT JSON:
\begin{verbatim}
{
  "therapist_skills": {"understanding": 0, "interpersonal_effectiveness": 0},
  "client_alliance": {"affective_bond": 0},
  "ctrs": {"collaboration": 0, "guided_discovery": 0, "focus": 0, "strategy": 0},
  "reasoning": "...",
  "comparative_advantage": "..."
}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\subsection*{Pairwise Preference Prompts (Layer 3, Judge 1: GPT-4o)}

The pairwise layer presents both complete sessions in a single prompt and forces a choice. Judge 1 is GPT-4o at temperature 0.0 with JSON mode enabled. Every comparison is judged twice with the presentation order of Sessions A and B swapped; a win is recorded only if the same session wins in both orders, otherwise the comparison is counted as a tie.

\begin{quote}\small
You are an expert psychological evaluator specializing in Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) and therapeutic alliance, following the evaluation framework of the MIRROR paper (arXiv:2504.13211) and the CTRS items of Young \& Beck (1980).

You will see TWO complete therapy sessions (Session A and Session B) conducted by two different AI therapists with comparable clients facing the same kind of life problem.

CRITICAL EVALUATION FOCUS:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Assess each therapist's ability to perceive ``Emotional Subtext'': the underlying feelings that may not be explicitly stated in the client's words but are hinted at through context and tone.
    \item A high-performing therapist moves beyond surface-level reflection, identifies ``Latent Concerns'', and facilitates deeper discovery.
    \item Assess general counseling skills (Understanding, Interpersonal Effectiveness, Collaboration), CBT-specific skills (Guided Discovery, Focus, Strategy for Change), and the affective bond with the client.
    \item Judge ONLY therapeutic quality. Do NOT reward longer responses; response length must not influence your decision.
    \item Evaluate each session as a whole, not individual turns.
\end{itemize}
\end{quote}

\begin{quote}\small
Two AI therapists (A and B) each conducted a full CBT session with a client facing the same kind of life problem. Decide which therapist better navigated the gap between what the client SAYS and what the client FEELS, and delivered the better session overall.

\begin{verbatim}
[Session A]
{SESSION_A}
[/Session A]

[Session B]
{SESSION_B}
[/Session B]
\end{verbatim}

Compare A and B on: Understanding, Interpersonal Effectiveness, Collaboration, Guided Discovery, Focus, Strategy for Change, and Affective Bond.

Return EXACT JSON:
\begin{verbatim}
{
  "winner": "A" or "B" or "tie",
  "reasoning": "..."
}
\end{verbatim}

Choose ``tie'' only if the two sessions are of genuinely equivalent quality.
\end{quote}

\subsection*{Cross-Family Second Judge (Claude Sonnet 4.6)}

The second judge re-scored a stratified subset of 100 comparisons (proportional allocation across the eight backbone--pair cells, random seed 42) under the identical protocol: the same system and user prompts as above, the same two-order swap rule, and temperature 0.0, called via OpenRouter under the model identifier \texttt{anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6}. The only procedural differences from Judge 1 are the model family and the absence of forced JSON mode (responses are parsed leniently). Post-swap final labels from the two judges yield the Cohen's $\kappa$ and directional-agreement statistics reported in Table~\ref{tab:kappa}.

